    "body": "# Minutes, 2025-08-22\n\n## Agenda\n## Attendees \nMason Freed, Dan Clark, Alex Keng, Olli Pettay, Leo Lee,  Joey Arhar, Domenic Denicola\n\n## Carryovers:\n[Should we Remove XSLT](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11523)\n\n## New topics:\nUpdates on [Proposal: Customized built-in elements via elementInternals.type #11061](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11061):\nMixin with Reactive Controllers pattern: https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11061#issuecomment-2673966134\nMixin with subclass factory pattern: https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11061#issuecomment-3000882088\n[Rendering select as a listbox is a one-line widget that opens a popup on iOS and Android · Issue #8189 · whatwg/html · GitHub](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/8189)\n[Add the interestfor attribute #11006](https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/11006)\n\n\n## Minutes:\n```\nStill tentative support for XSLT removal\nSome discussion of alternatives for customized built-in elements and API shapes\nThe [explainer](https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/MSEdgeExplainers/blob/main/ElementInternalsType/explainer.md) is updated with the static property approach per last week’s discussion.\nAlex: In terms of accessing built-in properties, mixin with Reactive Controllers pattern is similar to current internals.buttonMixin proposal where new built-in properties are exposed through a property of the custom element.\nOlli: Refactoring existing interfaces to HTMLButtonElement → HTMLButtonMixin → HTMLElement would not be web-compatible.\nNo prior art of exposing built-in subclass factory\nDomenic: Exposing the new functionality on elementInternals (and requiring developers to manually expose things on the outer element) would fit the way this kind of thing has been done in the past.\nDomenic: may be good to expose utility functions to help authors write wrappers\nDomenic will comment in issue\nSome discussion of <select> popup and in-page mobile/desktop behavior\n```",
